============================================================
(d) Sitewide axe, zero violations, both themes — FAIL
============================================================
Ran the worker's own axe_probe.js unmodified against my :8092 instance:
zero violations reported, all 9 pages, both themes, including the two
dashboard-modal passes. On its own terms this looked clean.

However, per the checker mandate to do my OWN live render/probe rather
than trust the worker's harness, I built an independent reveal strategy
and cross-checked specific real (non-hacked) user interactions the
worker's reveal script does not exercise. This surfaced THREE real,
axe-confirmed violations, all within the worker's own declared ruleset
(wcag2a/2aa/21aa/22aa) and all reachable via completely ordinary user
actions at the standard 1440px viewport — no CSS overrides needed for the
final confirmation runs:

1. /whatif — 3 of 5 results tabs (Cash Flow, Risk, Taxes & RMD) each
   contain a `scrollable-region-focusable` violation (serious, WCAG 2.1.1,
   tag wcag2a), both light and dark. Reached by simply clicking the
   visible tab buttons ([data-wf-tab="cashflow"|"risk"|"taxes"]) — no
   disclosure hack. Confirmed structurally: e.g. the Cash Flow
   projection-breakdown table reports scrollHeight=2416 vs
   clientHeight=384, tabindex=null — genuinely non-scrollable by keyboard.
   Root cause: axe_probe.js's reveal step only opens <details>,
   [data-wf-collapse-body], [aria-expanded="false"], and
   [data-quick-adjust-tab-panel] — it never clicks the top-level
   [data-wf-tab] results tabs, so 4 of 5 tab panels on /whatif (Cash Flow,
   Risk, Taxes & RMD, Strategies) were never shown to axe in the worker's
   own sitewide run. This is the SAME false-negative failure class the
   worker explicitly diagnosed and fixed for Quick Adjust in this very
   task, recurring in a second, larger, un-enumerated surface.
   git diff master: the scrollable-container markup (`overflow-x-auto` /
   `max-h-96 overflow-y-auto`, no tabindex) is byte-identical to master in
   tax-summary.html and projection-breakdown.html — pre-existing, not
   introduced by this diff. Noted for provenance, but U15's sole
   acceptance criterion (d) is exactly "axe zero violations sitewide,
   both themes" — this is a final, sitewide sweep task, not an
   incidental-touch task, so a real, axe-confirmed, ordinarily-reachable
   violation on one of the 9 named pages is in scope regardless of when
   the underlying markup was written.

2. /explorer — `target-size` (serious, WCAG 2.5.8, wcag22aa) on
   #clear-search-btn: 16x16px, only 16px of clear space to its neighbor
   (input), both under the 24x24 minimum. Reached by simply typing one
   character into the search box (the button is `hidden` until
   `.Search`/input has a value) — again a plain user action the worker's
   probe never performs. git diff master: this button's classes/icon size
   are byte-identical to master — pre-existing, but same in-scope
   reasoning as above.

3. /whatif — Healthcare Costs card's "+ Add Person" form (5 fields:
   person select, name, age, coverage select, monthly cost): dark mode
   `color-contrast` (serious) 2.77:1 vs required 4.5:1 (#000000 on
   #4b5563). Reached via a REAL click on the visible
   `[data-toggle-add-healthcare]` "+ Add Person" button (confirmed
   `add-healthcare-form` becomes visible, not a CSS hack). Root cause:
   this toggle button carries no `aria-expanded` attribute at all (unlike
   the settings-rail disclosures the probe does handle), so axe_probe.js's
   reveal selectors never open it. This task's own diff DID touch these
   exact lines (adding label/id associations, ruling d's own stated goal)
   but did not add a dark-mode text color to the inputs the way every
   sibling "Add X" form in the same file set does (income-sources-list.html
   and expense-sources-list.html's Add forms explicitly carry
   `dark:bg-gray-700 dark:text-gray-100`; this one carries `dark:bg-gray-600`
   with no `dark:text-*` at all) — an isolated omission, not a systemic
   one (checked: only healthcare-card.html uses the
   `data-toggle-add-*`-without-aria-expanded pattern).

All three were independently re-verified via real click-paths (not my
brute-force probe) before being counted, to rule out reveal-script
artifacts; two candidate findings from the brute-force pass (#file-input
label/contrast, #restore-file-input contrast) were investigated and
DISCARDED as false positives — both are `class="hidden"` native file
inputs that are NEVER meant to be visible under any real code path
(always programmatically `.click()`-triggered by a separate, properly
labelled visible button); forcing them visible via a global CSS override
is not a real user-reachable state and axe's findings there don't apply.

The manifest's verification section states "0 violations, all 9 pages,
both themes" for the sitewide axe pass. That claim is not accurate: real,
axe-confirmed, ordinarily-reachable violations exist on /whatif (x2
distinct causes) and /explorer, all inside the worker's own declared
ruleset, none requiring anything beyond a normal tab click, a keystroke
in a search box, or a normal disclosure click.

Nav-contrast backlog item (manifest's own disclosure): independently
re-confirmed at four widths (390/1024/1280/1440) — the /filemanager
"Setup" nav-group color-contrast finding reproduces ONLY at 1024px and is
absent at 390, 1280, and 1440. This is correctly out of scope for U15 (not
one of its named surfaces, and outside the 1440/390 standard viewports) —
treated as a backlog OBSERVATION, not a FAIL, per the task instructions.

